AB  - <title>Abstract</title> <p> Chitinase is a key virulence factor in entomopathogenic fungi, enabling host invasion through degradation of the chitin-rich insect cuticle. This study evaluated the role of chitinase produced by a native isolate of <italic>Beauveria bassiana</italic> (Bals.) Vuill. (Hypocreales; Cordycipitaceae) against the rice earhead bug, <italic>Leptocorisa oratorius</italic> (Fab.) (Hemiptera: Alydidae). Laboratory bioassays showed stage-dependent susceptibility, with first-instar nymphs exhibiting the highest mortality (84%) at 9 days after treatment, while egg hatchability was reduced to 72%, indicating partial ovicidal activity. Chitinase production was optimized under different cultural and environmental conditions, with maximum activity observed on the fifth day of incubation. Peptone (0.07%), colloidal chitin (0.4%), and FeSO₄ (0.015%) significantly enhanced enzyme production. Optimal fungal growth occurred at pH 5 and 28°C, whereas maximum chitinase activity was recorded at pH 5 and 37°C. Partial purification by ammonium sulfate fractionation resulted in the highest specific activity (2.60 U mg⁻¹), purification fold (2.68), and yield (14.34%) at 75% saturation. SDS-PAGE analysis revealed a molecular weight of approximately 60 kDa. The results confirm the importance of chitinase in <italic>B. bassiana</italic> pathogenicity and highlight its potential for developing eco-friendly biopesticides for sustainable management of rice insect pests. </p>
