AB  - <title>Abstract</title> <p> Southern corn rust (SCR), caused by <italic>Puccinia polysora</italic> , is a destructive foliar disease that threatens maize production worldwide. Excessive dependence on synthetic chemical fungicides has driven urgent demand for eco-friendly biocontrol alternatives based on beneficial microorganisms. In this study, two endophytic fungal isolates, <italic>Purpureocillium lilacinum</italic> PL6 and <italic>Beauveria bassiana</italic> YC-1, were recovered from maize leaves and evaluated as dual-purpose biocontrol candidates in maize using morphological observation, molecular phylogenetic identification, whole-genome sequencing, indoor bioassays, and insect pathogenicity tests. Illumina-based genome assembly yielded 36.68 Mb and 33.05 Mb draft for strains PL6 and YC-1, respectively. Functional annotation against the COG and KEGG databases revealed that both genomes are enriched in functional genes linked to carbohydrate, amino acid and energy metabolism, consistent with the typical metabolic plasticity of entomopathogenic fungi. In planta inoculation assays verified that root drench application of PL6 and YC-1 significantly suppressed SCR progression: at 24 days post pathogen inoculation, disease incidence dropped to below 4% for PL6 treatment and approximately 21% for YC-1 treatment, respectively, whereas the untreated control reached nearly 48%. Meanwhile, both strains exhibited pathogenicity against fifth-instar larvae of <italic>Spodoptera frugiperda</italic> , with YC-1 showing relatively higher larvicidal activity than PL6, but no statistically significant inter-strain divergence was detected for <italic>S. frugiperda</italic> lethality. These results demonstrate that endophytic PL6 and YC-1 have potential as genome-informed, dual-purpose fungal resources for integrated management of foliar rust disease and lepidopteran pests. The assembled genomic resources also facilitate subsequent genome-mining of functional genes for developing high-efficiency fungi. </p>
