AB  - Aerial conidia of 16 Beauveria bassiana strains originally derived from different host insects worldwide were separately suspended in a nutritional solution containing 2% sucrose and 0.5% peptone. For each of the strains, the conidial suspension of 1 mL in a 10-mL glass tube was exposed to a thermal stress of water bath at 48℃ for 45 min and then incubated by shaking at 25℃ for 24 h. The residue viability of each strain after the thermal stress was assessed as an index of its thermotolerance. As a result, mean residue viabilities in three assays, ranging from 4.6 to 87.1%, differed significantly among the 16 strains after exposure to the thermal stress. Of those, three strains were of high thermotolerance due to their residue viabilities of >40% but four were weak in thermotolerance due to their residue viabilities of <10%. The residue viabilities of the rest strains fell in a range of 11.0--23.6% and their thermotolerance was thus intermediate. When three strains with the residue viabilities of 40.8%, 15.8% and 4.6% after the thermal stress were grown separately on SDAY plates at 25, 30 and 35℃, their colony diameters were well in accordance with the level of the thermotolerance. Only was the most thermotolerant strain capable of growing and sporulating at 35℃, being unusual for B. bassiana, although colony diameters at 25℃ were not different among the three strains.
