AB  - Hydrolysis of (±)-β-aryl-γ-ethylidene-γ-lactones by fungal strain <i>Aspergillus ochraceus</i> AM370 afforded (−)-(<i>S</i>)-γ-ethylidene-γ-lactones <b>2a</b>⁻<b>d</b> and (+)-(<i>R</i>)-γ-ketoacids <b>3a</b>⁻<b>d</b>. Enantiomeric purity of the unreacted lactones was strictly related to a size of an aryl substituent at C-4 of γ-lactone ring, with the highest <i>ee</i> (77%) obtained for the (−)-(<i>S</i>)-γ-ethylidene-γ-lactone possessing unsubstituted benzene ring (<b>2a</b>) and the lowest one (15%) determined for the (−)-(<i>S</i>)-γ-ethylidene-γ-lactone with bulky 1,3-benzodioxole system (<b>2d</b>). Lactones <b>2a</b>⁻<b>d</b>, both racemic and enantiomerically enriched, as well as products of their hydrolysis showed varying degrees of feeding deterrent activity against lesser mealworm, <i>Alphitobius diaperinus</i> Panzer, which depended on the structure of the compound and the developmental stage of the lesser mealworm. In the case of adults, more active were γ-lactones <b>2a</b>⁻<b>d</b>, compared with ketoacids <b>3a</b>⁻<b>d</b>. Only in the case of lactone <b>2a</b> was the effect of configuration of stereogenic center on the activity found. Particularly strong deterrents against this stage (T > 180) were racemic and (−)-(<i>S</i>)-γ-ethylidene-γ-lactone with <i>p</i>-methoxysubstituted phenyl ring (<b>2c</b>).
