AB  - The carmine spider mite, Tetranchus cinnabarinus, is an economically important pest that devastates variedties of crops worldwide and develops significant resisitance to common chemical pesticides, most of which lack ovidicdal cactivity. In the present study , two isolated of entomopathogenic fungi, Beuaveria bassiana SG8702 and Paeilomycels fumosoroseus Pfr 153, were bioassayed against T. cinnabarius eggs at 25℃ under a photophase of 12:12(L:D). Infected eggs on Vicia faba var. minor leaves failed to hatch due to distortion adn shrinkage and had fungal outgrowths wen maintained under moist conditions. Sprays of B. bassiana onidia to T cinnabarinus eggs (on leaves )at the concentration of 58, 298 and 1306 conidia/mm~(2) (3 replicates per concentration and 35-68 fresh mite eggs per replicate) resulted in crrected gee mortailies of 20.4±4.2%, 36.0±7.6% and 64.6±12.5% (F=43.14, P<0.01), respectively; sprays of P. fumosooseus at 129, 402 and 2328 conidia/mm~(2) caused egg mortalities of 16.1±11.1%, 44.2±13.3%, and 63.4±11.7 (F=15.37, P=0.01), respectivley. In contrast, natural egg mrotalitie in blank controls were 7.8% adn 10.3% only. Based on probit anlaysis the estimate of LC_(50) with 95% confidental limits lwere 548 (393-858) conidia/mm~(2) for B. bassiana and 914(625-1550) for P. fumosorseus, respectively. Apparently, both fungal species wer ecapable of infecting T. cinabarinus eggs, bu the ovial activity of the B. bassiana isolate was greater than that of the P. fumosorseus isolate. THis represents the first regoport on the ovidcidal actiivty of the two entomopthogenic Hyphomcetes against T. cinabarius.
